const faqItems = [
  { question: "What does LD 1897 (P.L. 2025, ch. 514) actually change for Maine caregivers?", answer: "LD 1897, signed January 11, 2026 and effective July 29, 2026, creates a new caregiver cultivation category: the 'sun-grown cultivator.' A sun-grown caregiver who registers based on annual plant count may cultivate up to 150 mature cannabis plants, 300 immature plants, and unlimited seedlings between March and December. A sun-grown caregiver who registers based on annual canopy may cultivate up to 2,500 square feet of mature plant canopy and 5,000 square feet of immature plant canopy between March and December. The base caregiver limit of 30 mature plants / 500 square feet of mature canopy remains in effect for indoor and mixed-light cultivators." },
  { question: "When does the 150-plant / 2,500-sq-ft option become available?", answer: "The new option takes effect on July 29, 2026, the general effective date for non-emergency laws passed in the Second Regular Session of the 132nd Legislature. Caregivers who wish to register as sun-grown cultivators under the new tier will need to update their registration with the Office of Cannabis Policy for the 2026 growing season. Caregivers already cultivating under the existing 30-plant limit may continue to do so; the new tier is opt-in, not mandatory." },
  { question: "What is the legal definition of 'sun-grown cultivation' under the amendment?", answer: "Amended 22 M.R.S. §2423-A(2)(B-1) defines 'outdoor cultivation area' (the operational term the bill uses; 'sun-grown cultivator' is the registration term) as 'a cultivation area that primarily uses sunlight for the cultivation of mature cannabis plants.' The statute does not require 100% natural light — supplemental lighting during the seedling or immature phase, or during weather events, is not excluded — but the dominant lighting source for mature plants must be sunlight. A greenhouse with significant HPS or LED supplemental lighting during flower would not qualify; a high tunnel, a Hoop house, or an open-air outdoor cultivation site would." },
  { question: "Can a sun-grown caregiver also cultivate indoors under the 30-plant base limit?", answer: "No. The amendment is explicit that a sun-grown cultivator 'may not cultivate cannabis under paragraph B' — the 30-plant base — and a caregiver cultivating under the base 30-plant rule 'may not cultivate cannabis under paragraph B-1' (the new 150-plant outdoor option). It is one or the other, not both. A caregiver who wants the operational flexibility of both an indoor and an outdoor cultivation area would need to register two separate caregiver registrations or transition to a cultivation facility license through the Office of Cannabis Policy." },
  { question: "What is the registration fee for a sun-grown caregiver?", answer: "Amended 22 M.R.S. §2425-A(10)(B) sets the annual fee for a sun-grown cultivator at $50 to $240 for each group of 30 mature plants, with a 150-plant cap (so a fully-utilized sun-grown registration is $600 to $2,400 per year for the plant-count track). For the canopy track, the fee is $50 to $1,500 for up to 2,500 square feet of mature canopy. The fee is scaled by the OCP based on the caregiver's reported cultivation footprint. The base 30-plant caregiver registration remains $240 per year for the plant-count track and $1,500 for the canopy track." },
  { question: "How does the sun-grown option interact with caregiver retail sales?", answer: "A sun-grown caregiver is still a caregiver under the Maine Medical Use of Cannabis Act, with all the same patient-facing rights and obligations. The 150-plant annual limit applies to mature cannabis plants in flower. A sun-grown caregiver can sell harvested product to registered qualifying patients through the same channels every caregiver uses: the registered premises, delivery, private arrangement, and (after July 29, 2026) at trade shows and industry events under LD 1840. The annual gross sales figure for a fully-utilized sun-grown caregiver is materially higher than for a 30-plant base caregiver; OCP's reporting and tax requirements are unchanged." },
  { question: "What security and reporting requirements apply to a 150-plant outdoor cultivation site?", answer: "Amended 22 M.R.S. §2423-A(3)(B) requires an outdoor cultivation area to include 'reasonable precautions to prevent unauthorized access, such as a gated perimeter, fencing, locked gates, or natural barriers combined with signage.' The statute gives the caregiver discretion on the specific perimeter security, but the OCP has inspection authority and may require upgrades if the site is not reasonably secured. The caregiver must still report plant counts in Metrc, may not cultivate immature plants by canopy if cultivating mature plants by plant count (and vice versa), and remains subject to the 8-plant limit per registered qualifying patient under the standard caregiver rule." }
];
